From 29c125ce273fb6b3e0f1a222daf9cbca69cd9814 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:23:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Subtitles] Dim bitmap subtitles in light, and the image
 subtitle settings in Spanish

A white PGS or VobSub subtitle is drawn at the full white of the output, and on a dark film
that is what the eye goes to: "lifesaver" was what a user called a way to dim it. Kodi has
nothing for it; this tree's brightness of the menus in HDR (videoscreen.hadesguiwhite) moves
the menus and text subtitles too, and leaves a subtitle authored in HDR at its own levels.

A new setting in the "Image based subtitles" group (Player > Subtitles) that cinemaONE's
bitmap zoom and position bring: subtitles.hadesbitmapbrightness, 10 to 100 % in steps of 5,
100 by default. It scales the light of the subtitle, not its code values: each colour of the
palette (or each pixel of an RGBA bitmap, or the eight colours of a DVD's SPU, before their
alpha is merged) is decoded with the sRGB curve, or with ST 2084 where the subtitle is in HDR
and stays so, multiplied, and encoded again. So 50 % is half the light whatever the output: on
SDR, and on HDR10 or native Dolby Vision, where the menus and subtitles are composed into the
picture afterwards and keep their proportions. It is done once, when the texture of a
subtitle is made, and a change of the setting makes the textures again (ReleaseCache()).

Only what cinemaONE's first commit marks as a bitmap subtitle is dimmed: PGS and VobSub
decoded by FFmpeg, and a DVD's SPU that is not forced. Disc menus (Blu-ray IG and BD-J, a
DVD's button highlights), DVB (which also carries logos) and text are not. At 100 % nothing
of this runs and the pixels are those of before, bit for bit.

The labels and help of the group, cinemaONE's settings included, are in Spanish where Hades
has its other settings in Spanish: through Estuary (#32280-#32286, en_gb, es_es, es_ar,
es_mx) and CGUIUtils::GetHadesString(), as the other settings of Hades.
